# Typography in context

Type on a dashboard is mostly readings, names and states that people check many times a day. Set them so the number, its unit and how recent it is can be read at a glance, on any screen and on paper.

[Typography](typography.html) owns the typeface, the weights and the type scale. This page explains how to use them in each place a household meets civica text.

## Text in each context

This comparison shows the typeface and the smallest text size to use in each place civica text appears.

| Context | Typeface | Smallest text | Who controls it |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| civica card or panel on a dashboard | Arial | 16px supporting text | The civica theme |
| Native Home Assistant screen | Arial | 12px compact extra text | Home Assistant, with civica sizes |
| Chart label or legend | Arial | 14px | The civica chart cards |
| Companion app settings, keyboard and share sheet | The phone's own font | Set by the phone | The phone's operating system |
| Printed household handover | Arial | 12pt body text | The person printing it |
| Social image or release picture | Arial | Large enough to read on a phone | The person making it |

Every screen size in this table grows with Home Assistant's font size scale and with browser or app zoom.

This chart compares the smallest screen text in each context. Notice that 3 contexts have no bar: their smallest size is set by the phone, measured in points on paper, or not fixed, and none of them is 0.

<%=
  hex = ->(id) { govuk_colour(id).fetch('hex') }
  ink, grid, blue = hex.('black'), hex.('black-tint-80'), hex.('blue')
  contexts = [
    ['civica card or panel', 16, nil],
    ['Native Home Assistant screen', 12, nil],
    ['Chart label or legend', 14, nil],
    ['Companion app settings', nil, 'Set by the phone'],
    ['Printed household handover', nil, '12pt on paper, not on this scale'],
    ['Social image or release picture', nil, 'Not fixed: large enough to read on a phone']
  ]
  x_of = ->(px) { 4 + px * 12 }
  svg = []
  [0, 4, 8, 12, 16].each { |v| svg << %(<line x1="#{x_of.(v)}" y1="4" x2="#{x_of.(v)}" y2="208" stroke="#{grid}" stroke-width="1"/><text x="#{x_of.(v)}" y="222" font-size="10" text-anchor="middle" fill="#{ink}">#{v}</text>) }
  svg << %(<text x="#{x_of.(16)}" y="236" font-size="10" text-anchor="middle" fill="#{ink}">Smallest text (px)</text>)
  contexts.each_with_index do |(name, px, note), i|
    y = 16 + i * 33
    svg << %(<text x="4" y="#{y}" font-size="11" font-weight="700" fill="#{ink}">#{name}</text>)
    svg << (px ? %(<rect x="4" y="#{y + 5}" width="#{px * 12}" height="12" fill="#{blue}"/><text x="#{x_of.(px) + 5}" y="#{y + 15}" font-size="10" fill="#{ink}">#{px}px</text>) : %(<text x="6" y="#{y + 15}" font-size="10" fill="#{ink}">No bar. #{note}</text>))
  end
  specimen = contexts.select { |c| c[1] }.map { |name, px, _| %(<p style="font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:#{px}px;line-height:1.5;margin:0 0 5px">Living room 21.4°C, updated 14:05 <span style="font-size:16px">(#{name}, #{px}px)</span></p>) }.join
  %(<div class="c-colour-example"><figure style="margin:0 0 20px">) +
