<h2 id="bg-typ-01">Use Arial everywhere civica sets the text</h2>

Use Arial on dashboards, in custom cards, in documentation, on printed handovers and in social images. Take the font stack and weights from [Typography](typography.html#typeface).

Do not restyle text that the phone draws itself. In the Home Assistant Companion app, dashboards use the civica theme. The app's own settings screens, the keyboard, the share sheet and permission requests use the phone's own font. Leave them as they are. They match the rest of the phone, and people already know how to use them.

<h2 id="bg-typ-02">Lay out text for reading</h2>

Left-align instructions, labels and help text, in English and in Welsh. A straight left edge makes long text quicker to read. Centre text only for a short, deliberate line, such as a large reading in the middle of a gauge.

Do not shorten a device name or an error message to fit a headline length. Let it wrap. [Typography](typography.html#headings) explains heading levels and how a reading differs from a heading.

This example shows a long room name that wraps, with a section heading and a reading under it.

<%= example('bg-type-household-hierarchy') %>

<h2 id="bg-typ-03">Keep readings, units and times together</h2>

Show a reading with its unit and the time it was taken. For example: “Living room 21°C, updated 14:05”. Keep the text selectable, so people can copy a reading.

| Do | Do not |
| --- | --- |
| −0.16 kW | 0.16 kW, when the house is exporting |
| 21.5°C | 21°C, when the half degree matters |
| Unavailable | 0°C, or a blank space |
| 18.2°C, updated 09:10 | 18.2°C, with no time, from a sensor that stopped reporting yesterday |

Never cut off the minus sign, the decimal part, the unit, the device name or a word such as “Unavailable” or “stale”. Use tabular numerals, `font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums`, where people compare readings in a column, so the digits line up.

Keep the language, number format, time format and time zone the household chose in Home Assistant. A written report says which period and time zone it covers when a reader could be unsure. [Charts and data](../content/formatting-content/charts-and-data.html) explains how to word numbers and times.

Do not put a changing reading inside an image. It cannot update, and a screen reader cannot read it.

This example shows power readings in a table. Notice the minus sign on the grid export, the unit on every value and the time of each reading.

<%= example('bg-type-values') %>

<h2 id="bg-typ-04">Keep native screens dense</h2>

Native Home Assistant screens, such as entity settings and the automation editor, show a lot of information. They keep the smaller native sizes in [the type scale](typography.html#native-home-assistant-sizes). Do not make every editor row the size of a heading.

Chart labels and legends in civica chart cards are at least 14px, and grow with the font size scale. Code editors keep a monospace font, because the alignment of code carries meaning.

These are civica settings for its own theme and cards. A card from another developer may not follow them.

<h2 id="bg-typ-05">Check text at larger sizes and in difficult states</h2>

Check every card with browser zoom and with a raised font size scale. Labels, errors and help text must get taller, not be cut off. Test with:

- a long room name, such as “Upstairs landing by the airing cupboard”
- a large reading, such as 1,284.6 kWh
- a reading with no unit, an Unavailable state and an empty list
- a Welsh or other translated label, which is often longer than the English

A tooltip only adds detail. Keyboard and touch users need the name and state without hovering. If a compact native control cuts off text that is not essential, keep the full name available to screen readers and give a way to see it by keyboard and touch. Do not turn off zoom or fix a card's height to hide text that does not fit.

[Test the interface](../accessibility/testing.html) gives the full zoom and reflow checks.

<h2 id="bg-typ-06">Set type for print and social images</h2>

For a printed household handover, use at least 12pt for body text and 16pt for headings. Use bigger text if the reader needs it. Print a test page and read it, because a page that looks fine on screen can be too small on paper. Use the household's own name for the document. It does not need a civica logo.

This example shows the start of a printed handover in Arial, with 16pt headings and 12pt body text.

<%= example('bg-type-handover') %>

For a social image or release picture, use a short headline that can be read on a phone. Put the same message in the post text, so people who cannot see the image get it too. Keep dates, links, readings and instructions in text, not in the picture. [Social colours](colour-social.html) gives the colours for social images.
